  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe ridge parked over the Central Plains keeps most of the\nremnants of Hurricane Isaias further east of our area. This\nridge will persist through the early part of next week and it\nwill slowly move east. Meanwhile, Hurricane Simon off the\nsouthwest coast of Mexico is forecast to bring us our next rain\nchances for the middle of next week. Models have Hurricane Simon\nmaking landfall on the west side of Mexico and seemingly gets\nstrung out due to our ridge. The remnants of Hurricane Simon are\nforecast to get caught up in the ridge and move right through\nsouthern MO. Rain chances increase Tuesday evening into\nWednesday morning with rain chances at 30-60% across the area\nwith the higher chances over central MO. Then, large mid-level\ntrough is expected to move through towards the end of next week\nand bring more rain and a stronger cold front. Thanks to the \nfrontal passage, those rain chances stick around through Friday \nwhen they start to taper off in the late morning. \n\nThis strong low pressure system will tighten the pressure \ngradient as early as Wednesday. This will cause southerly winds \nto become gusty up to 25 mph from Wednesday through Friday. \nThroughout the day Friday, the air mass will start to feel \ndifferent as dewpoints drop by almost 20 degrees and winds turn\nnortherly. Highs for next Friday are forecast to be in the \nlower 70s. However, this is a long term forecast and these \nnumbers are likely to change. The NBM high temperature spread \nfor Springfield for Friday is 65 to 78 degrees. With this large \nof a temperature spread of over 10 degrees, there is a lot of\nuncertainty and we will have to see how this air mass behaves \nas we get closer. Though, having a high temperature less than 90\ndegrees is a welcome change and this cold air advection hopefully\nkeeps itself together long enough to bring us a taste of fall \nby next weekend!",
